.TH LINE3TEST 1
.SH NAME
line3test \- check draw3d's GPU line provider against the software one
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B line3test
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Line3test
draws a fan of
.B line3
segments through both
.IR draw3d (2)
providers \(em the software one and the
.BR /dev/draw -backed
.B draw3ddev
\(em and reports how many segments each actually rendered, followed by
.B PASS
or a
.B FAIL
naming how many went missing.
.PP
It counts
.I segments,
not lit pixels.
A pixel count cannot distinguish sixty thin segments from twenty thick
ones, which is exactly the failure this exists to catch: each spoke is
projected to where it is expected to appear and probed there.
.PP
The GPU line hook only accepts the screen as a destination, so the fan
is drawn to the display image and read back.
That is reliable rather than racy \(em
.IR draw (3)
runs any queued GPU geometry before serving a read, so the readback
observes work that has not been presented yet.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
% line3test
line3: software 60/60, draw3ddev 60/60
PASS
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/line3test.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw3d (2),
.IR draw3ddemo (1)
.SH BUGS
It exercises the working path.
The failure it was written for only appears when a Metal geometry pass
cannot run and the software fallback takes over, which this cannot
provoke from Limbo; reproducing that needs
.B metal_present_geom
in
.I emu/MacOSX/win-cocoa.m
made to fail.
Passing here does not prove the fallback path is sound.
